    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/10/brooks-hyperion-3-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: DNA Flash v2 is softer with higher stack, giving better impact protection, but ground feel/forefoot flexibility are reduced and the large heel flare can make heel landings feel clunky or slappy.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is moderate rather than lively; reviewers found it less bouncy/snappy than Hyperion 2, workable for easy-to-tempo efforts but not ideal for fast strides or top-speed work.",
        "Stability: Torsional/overall rigidity is mild-to-moderate in a neutral platform; a rigid, heavily padded heel counter adds rearfoot structure but can push the foot forward and create awkward toe-off/lateral comfort issues for some.",
        "Durability: High outsole rubber coverage gives solid traction (including wet roads) with only mild wear after ~25 miles, and overall durability is expected to be average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/08/brooks-hyperion-3-mulit-tester-review-6.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The DNA Flash v2 midsole is described as soft and bouncy, and the higher 36/28 mm stack plus a thicker sockliner delivers noticeable forefoot comfort and strong impact protection without feeling overdone.",
        "Responsiveness: The ride is lively and energetic (especially at uptempo to half-marathon pace), but it is reported as not quite as snappy as some competitors, with reduced forefoot pop from softer front rubber.",
        "Stability: Although a neutral trainer, it feels stable from the widened platform, excellent midfoot/heel lockdown, and a rigid heel counter with substantial padding; firmer forefoot rubber could further improve front-end/lateral control.",
        "Durability: Long-term outsole wear is not deeply documented, but the upper construction looks robust (thick jacquard mesh, substantial toe bumper/heel structure), while comments about needing firmer forefoot rubber suggest possible front outsole longevity tradeoffs."
      ]
    }
